The K run repeated after the changes gave prove the same scores, class
by class: these formulas are limited by the worlds they need, not by
the speed of the search.

No prover gave a wrong answer.  prove is far behind on everything but
the pigeonhole classes (ph), which are mostly propositional and where
its clause-learning search matches or passes the others; elsewhere it
stops within the first few formulas, out of time or out of worlds (more
than 2048, when it leaves the question open rather than guess).  The
reason is its encoding: every possibility gets a world of its own at
every world where it is met (Km2SAT, Sebastiani and Vescovi 2009, here
in negation normal form, one way, statements grounded asserted and
denied), and the LWB formulas, with dozens of operators nested deep,
ask for more worlds than that allows.  Spartacus reuses worlds whose
formulas it has seen (caching) and builds them only as its search needs
them; KSP needs no worlds at all.  prove's arguments, a few operators
nested two or three deep, are far below where this bites; a modal
argument of LWB's size wants those provers, which prove -t can feed.

The encoding before this one (a fixed tree of worlds, its branching
the count of every operator) did worse: it decided the second formula
of no class, and on KD arguments it had cut short it once called
premises contradictory that were not (kd-056, kd-102), which the
benchmark's scoring then hid; a frame cut short now decides only a
refutation, never a proof.

Where prove breaks (stress.py; each family grows until it fails, one
emulator a file, 120 seconds, 1 GB):

	family			fine			slow			out of time
	independent premises	1600 (0.4 s)		6400 (6.1 s)		25600
	chain of implications	1600 (0.7 s)		6400 (8.7 s)		-
	random 3-CNF, atoms	75 (3.2 s)		100 (56 s)		150
	steps, each on the last	3200 (2.5 s)		-			-
	formula nested deep	100000 (0.6 s)		-			-
	objections and replies	3200 (1.0 s)		-			-
	one-place predicates	8 (0.05 s)		-			-

No wrong answer and no crash in any of them; what fails is time.  The
first run of this table broke at a quarter of these sizes (premises at
1600 in 24 s, chains at 1600 in 27 s, steps at 800 in 12 s, objections
at 800 in 2.6 s), and was mended without changing any output: every
argument here and in gen.py's set, 953 in all, gives the same findings
byte for byte, its TPTP too, as before.  What it took:

- every question had been sized to the whole argument (each "is P1 a
  tautology?" built a solver for every atom); now to the atoms of its
  own formulas, numbered in their order.  An atom no clause holds is
  only ever decided true, at a level nothing follows from, so the
  search and the case it finds are the same.
- the explanations (what is needed, what contradicts, what is missing)
  ask one solver many questions under assumptions (MiniSat's), each
  premise behind a guard of its own; they answer only yes or no, a fact
  of logic, so the same as before.  Unsatisfiable cores, which would ask
  fewer, are not used: they can pick a different smallest set, and the
  findings would change.
- the next variable to decide was found by looking from the first at
  every decision; now from where every one before is known to be set.
  The same variable, so the same search: kd-105, 25394 variables, 84 s
  to 25 s.
- atoms, predicates and names found by hash; the grounded labelling of
  objections over attacks indexed once (it is the least the rules
  allow, whatever order it is found in).

The whole account, with what was tried and turned down, and the check
any further change must pass (same.sh), is docs/PROVE-PERFORMANCE.md.

What is left is in the nature of the method.  What is needed is found
by dropping each premise in turn and asking again, which is the square
of the premises (5 s of the 6 at 6400 independent premises); the order
it drops them in decides which smallest set is reported, and a faster
method could report another.  The ordered search that finds the
plainest case repeats its decisions after each conflict (kd-105: 670
conflicts, 750000 decisions a question).  The random 3-CNF is a search
no argument needs; industrial solvers' restarts and heuristics would
carry it further, at the cost of the plainest case.
